1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Test 2

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is the IELTS listening test.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
All the recordings will be played once only.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The test is in four parts.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
At the end of the test, you will be given ten minutes to transfer your answers to the answer sheet.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Now turn to part one.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Part one.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
You will hear a man asking about voluntary work in the village that he has just moved to.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
First, you have some time to look at questions one to seven.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Now listen carefully and answer questions one to seven.

13
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Hello, Jane Fairbank speaking.

14
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Oh, good morning. My name's Frank Pritchard. I've just retired and moved to Southo.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
I'd like to become a volunteer and I gather you coordinate voluntary work in the village.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
That's right.

17
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
What sort of thing could I do?

18
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Well, we need help with the village library.

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
We borrow books from the town library and individuals also donate them.

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
So one thing you could do is get involved in collecting them, if you've got a car, that is.

21
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Yes, that's no problem.

22
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The times are pretty flexible, so we can arrange it to suit you.

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Oh, another thing is the records that we keep of the books we're given

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and those we borrow and need to return to the town library.

25
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
It would be very useful to have another person to help keep them up to date.

26
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Right. I'm used to working on a computer. I presume their computerised?

27
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Oh, yes.

28
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Is the library purpose built? I haven't noticed it when I've walked around the village.

29
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
No, we simply have the use of a room in the village hall, the west room.

30
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
It's on the left as you go in.

31
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
I must go and have a look inside the hall.

32
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Yes, it's a nice building.

33
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Do you run a lunch club in the village for elderly people? I know a lot of places do.

34
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Yes, we have a very successful club.

35
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
I could help with transport, if that's of any use.

36
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Oh, definitely. People come to the club from neighbouring villages

37
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
and we're always in need of more drivers.

38
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
And does the club have groups that focus on a particular hobby too?

39
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
I could get involved in one or two, particularly if there are any art groups.

40
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Excellent. I'll find out where we need help and get back to you.

41
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Fine. What about help for individual residents? Do you arrange that at all?

42
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Yes, we do it as a one-off.

43
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Oh, in fact, there's Mrs Carol. She needs a lift to the hospital next week

44
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
and we're struggling to find someone.

45
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
When's her appointment?

46
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
On Tuesday. It would take the whole morning.

47
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,000
I could do that?

48
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Oh, that would be great. Thank you.

49
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
And also next week, we're arranging to have some work done to Mr Selbury's house

50
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
before he moves, as he isn't healthy enough to do it himself.

51
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
We've got some people to decorate his kitchen,

52
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
but if you could do some weeding in his garden, that would be wonderful.

53
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Okay, I'd enjoy that. And presumably the day and time are flexible.

54
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Oh, yes. Just say when would suit you best and we'll let Mr Selbury know.

55
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Good.

56
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Now listen and answer questions eight to ten.

57
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
The Volunteers Group also organises monthly social events,

58
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
which is a great way to meet other people, of course.

59
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
So next month, on the 19th of October, we're holding a quiz.

60
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
A couple of residents are great at planning unusual ones,

61
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
and we always fill the village hall.

62
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,000
That sounds like fun. Can I do anything to help?

63
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Well, because of the number of people, we need plenty of refreshments for halfway through.

64
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
So if you could provide any, we'd be grateful.

65
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
I'm sure I could. I'll think about what to make and let you know.

66
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Thank you.

67
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Then on November the 18th, we're holding a dance, also in the village hall.

68
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
We've booked a band that specialises in music of the 1930s.

69
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
They've been before, and we've had a lot of requests to bring them back.

70
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
I'm not really a dancer, but I'd like to do something to help.

71
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Well, we sell tickets in advance, and having an extra person to check them at the door

72
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
as people arrive would be good.

73
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
It can be quite a bottleneck if everyone arrives at once.

74
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Okay, I'm happy with that.

75
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
We're also arranging a New Year's Eve party.

76
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
We're expecting that to be a really big event,

77
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
so instead of the village hall, it'll be held in the Mountfort Hotel.

78
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
The...

79
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Mountfort. M-O-U-N-T-F-O-R-T Hotel.

80
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
It isn't in Southo itself, but it's only a couple of miles away.

81
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
The hotel will be providing dinner, and we've booked a band.

82
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
The one thing we haven't got yet is a poster.

83
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
That isn't something you could do by any chance, is it?

84
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Well, actually, yes.

85
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Before I retired, I was a graphic designer, so that's right up my street.

86
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Oh, perfect.

87
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
I'll give you the details, and then perhaps you could send me a draft?

88
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Of course.

89
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
That is the end of Part One.

90
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
You now have half a minute to check your answers to Part One.

91
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
You

